From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 04:27:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: exynos9820: Add CMU_DPU and the DPU
 System MMUs

The display pipeline sits behind three System MMUs. They are SysMMU v7,
which the existing exynos-iommu driver already handles; all they need is
a device tree description.

Addresses, interrupts and the clock of every SysMMU come from the vendor
device tree (LineageOS/android_kernel_samsung_exynos9820, commit
7d08ec364a06, arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dts):
sysmmu@190A0000 with interrupts 212/211, sysmmu@190B0000 with 214/213
and sysmmu@190C0000 with 216/215, where the first interrupt of each pair
is the non-secure one. Only the non-secure interrupt is described, as
the mainline driver does not use the secure one.

The clock controller node comes with them. Its window is the CMU_DPU base
of the vendor register map, and its input is the DPU bus clock CMU_TOP
divides down; it sits outside the power domain switch because the domain
cannot be brought up without it.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi | 46 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
index db984630c4c..f2ec573f675 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
@@ -461,6 +461,52 @@ pinctrl_aud: pinctrl@18c60000 {
 			reg = <0x18c60000 0x1000>;
 		};
 
+		cmu_dpu: clock-controller@19000000 {
+			compatible = "samsung,exynos9820-cmu-dpu";
+			reg = <0x19000000 0x8000>;
+			#clock-cells = <1>;
+
+			clocks = <&oscclk>,
+				 <&cmu_top CLK_DOUT_CMU_DPU_BUS>;
+			clock-names = "oscclk", "bus";
+			power-domains = <&pd_dpu>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * One system MMU per display data path.  All three have to be
+		 * listed by the controller, because a frame is fetched through
+		 * whichever of them the window happens to use.
+		 */
+		sysmmu_dpud0: iommu@190a0000 {
+			compatible = "samsung,exynos-sysmmu";
+			reg = <0x190a0000 0x9000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 212 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&cmu_dpu CLK_GOUT_DPU_SYSMMU_DPUD0_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "sysmmu";
+			power-domains = <&pd_dpu>;
+			#iommu-cells = <0>;
+		};
+
+		sysmmu_dpud1: iommu@190b0000 {
+			compatible = "samsung,exynos-sysmmu";
+			reg = <0x190b0000 0x9000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 214 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&cmu_dpu CLK_GOUT_DPU_SYSMMU_DPUD1_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "sysmmu";
+			power-domains = <&pd_dpu>;
+			#iommu-cells = <0>;
+		};
+
+		sysmmu_dpud2: iommu@190c0000 {
+			compatible = "samsung,exynos-sysmmu";
+			reg = <0x190c0000 0x9000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 216 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&cmu_dpu CLK_GOUT_DPU_SYSMMU_DPUD2_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "sysmmu";
+			power-domains = <&pd_dpu>;
+			#iommu-cells = <0>;
+		};
+
 		/*
 		 * Every other clock controller takes its input from this one, so it
 		 * is registered before them.
